WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:08.070
As mentioned, the focus here will be on using Epic Games' own Reality Capture photogrammetry solution.

2
00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:15.890
However, let's spare a thought for AliceVision's Meshroom, which is a free application also available for download.

3
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:23.390
Meshroom is a very solid solution for photogrammetry work, although it is a bit slower than other dedicated applications.

4
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:29.620
What's more, it contains many templates that take you beyond photogrammetry work.

5
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:37.399
For example, it has a template that allows you to stitch pictures together to create 360-degree panoramas.

6
00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:44.939
When generating 3D models from pictures, though, it tends to generate too many textures to make the model practical.

7
00:00:44.939 --> 00:00:52.009
So there may be a bit of back and forth between Meshroom and your DCC application of choice to remedy that.

8
00:00:52.009 --> 00:00:58.770
All in all, it's worth a look if you're in the market for a solid and 100% free photogrammetry solution.

9
00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:03.770
But again, our focus will be on Reality Capture, developed by Epic Games.

10
00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.120
Reality Capture's workflow is easy.

11
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:16.459
Once you have taken the appropriate pictures, you load them in, you align them together and create point cloud data.

12
00:01:16.459 --> 00:01:27.739
You then build a mesh object from this data, possibly adjust that mesh by cleaning it up, decimating triangles, smoothing the geometry, etc.

13
00:01:27.739 --> 00:01:38.159
You apply the appropriate texturing from the available images, and export the model as a 3D mesh, typically as an FBX or OBJ file.

14
00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:45.019
This whole process is very simple, and often achieved with a few mouse clicks.

15
00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:56.390
At the time this video was created, the latest released version of Reality Capture is 1.4, and you can visit CapturingReality.com for information.

16
00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:04.890
Starting with version 1.4, Reality Capture is free to use if your company's annual revenue is less than $1 million.

17
00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:09.590
Beyond that, you would need an annual subscription plan.

18
00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:16.090
You can download the app directly from the website, or simply download and install it using the Epic Games launcher.

19
00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:29.490
The first time you launch Reality Capture, you are treated to a welcome or help page in a single pane interface.

20
00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:43.159
But Reality Capture has many layouts accessible from the top left corner.

21
00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:54.960
The two-pane layout is practical when viewing pictures in 2D mode, or when getting information about the project in 1D mode.

22
00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.000
The main large pane is useful to view the 3D information as you work on the project.

23
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:09.240
This two-pane layout is useful in most situations, but there are others that you may find more helpful in some cases.

24
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:20.960
To load images, you simply drag them from their location in File Explorer.

25
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.979
Once they appear in the project, you are ready to align them.

26
00:03:25.979 --> 00:03:31.120
This can be done in the Alignment tab or by pressing F6.

27
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.199
It takes a few moments to process, and a point cloud is created.

28
00:03:36.199 --> 00:03:45.629
Essentially, the software finds common or feature points between pictures, and recreates them in 3D in a point cloud formation.

29
00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:53.969
You can use the mouse wheel to zoom in and out the 3D view, and the right-click to orbit around the scene.

30
00:03:53.969 --> 00:03:58.090
Ctrl-left-click allows you to pan.

31
00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:05.090
In addition to the point cloud, you can see the cameras that were used to build the data, in this case all of them.

32
00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:10.360
There is also a bounding box that you can use to process the model.

33
00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:17.649
In the Tools menu, you can use the Set Ground Plane feature to reorient the model if need be.

34
00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:23.149
For that, you can use the numerical keypad to switch to an isometric view.

35
00:04:23.149 --> 00:04:33.310
The 2 key will switch you to a top view, 4 and 6 to a side view.

36
00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:50.509
The Set Reconstruction Region feature allows you to limit the calculations to a given area, thus removing unwanted sections.

37
00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:59.319
The 0 key takes you back to a perspective view.

38
00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:01.819
You are now ready to create a mesh model.

39
00:05:01.819 --> 00:05:08.720
This is done in the aptly named Mesh Model menu, or by using the F7 key.

40
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:16.670
Normal Detail Mode is generally enough, as it creates a model with detail in the order of a few million triangles.

41
00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:25.170
This process is the longest to calculate, and in this case will take about 3 to 5 minutes, depending on the speed of your computer.

42
00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:34.550
Once it's done, you can zoom in on the dino head to see it better.

43
00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:42.550
There are still a few rebel parts that we need to clean up, but first, switch the 2D pane into an Info or 1D Pane mode.

44
00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:57.079
The info on the pictures is still there, but more importantly, there is a component that lists a 3D model made of over 3 million triangles.

45
00:05:57.079 --> 00:06:10.660
Go to the Tools menu, and use the Lasso tool to select the superfluous triangles at the neck level.

46
00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:19.160
You can use the Ctrl key to add to the selection, and alternatively, you can also use the Rectangle tool to that effect if it's easier.

47
00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:33.069
When done, click the Filter Selection button to remove the selected triangles and clean up the model.

48
00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:40.069
Note, however, that this creates a new model entry in the component, thus keeping a history of your work.

49
00:06:40.069 --> 00:06:44.889
You can compare the models by clicking on the appropriate entry in the component.

50
00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:55.899
Both are well detailed with over 3 million triangles, but maybe you don't need this much.

51
00:06:55.899 --> 00:06:59.399
Use the Simplify tool to decimate that number.

52
00:06:59.399 --> 00:07:08.829
Set your target triangle count, say to about 300,000, which in this case is one tenth the original value.

53
00:07:08.829 --> 00:07:15.740
And process the Simplify function to bring the detail down.

54
00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:21.300
Note the new entry in the component, standing at 300,000 faces.

55
00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:29.209
If you zoom in, you may notice the triangulation is a bit rough.

56
00:07:29.209 --> 00:07:33.860
You can help it by smoothing it out a little.

57
00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:37.990
Exit the Simplify tool and enable the smoothing tool.

58
00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:51.029
You can experiment with different values, but I find that a strong type working on surface vertices and a weight value of 1 works best.

59
00:07:51.029 --> 00:07:58.029
Again, this adds a new model to the stack, and that will be our final one, so make sure it's selected.

60
00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:09.139
All that's left is to texture it, and you can use that feature in the Mesh Model tab or by pressing F9.

61
00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:15.939
A pop-up will indicate a feature still in development.

62
00:08:15.939 --> 00:08:19.439
You can opt to never display the warning again and close the window.

63
00:08:19.439 --> 00:08:26.079
Again, it will take a moment or two before it textures the dino head with data coming from the pictures.

64
00:08:26.079 --> 00:08:41.789
You now have a model ready for export, and you could use the Export feature in the application menu to save the model as an FBX or OBJ file.

65
00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:46.789
In the next video, we look at scanning a model from multiple sides.
